    "question": "How do you retain the selected state when an Activity is recreated after a configuration change?",
    "slug": "android-developer-how-do-you-retain-the-selected-state-when-an-activity-is-recreated-after-a-configuration-change-1",
    "shortDescription": "Reported Android interview question for junior / entry-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "When an Activity is recreated after a configuration change such\nas rotation, the Activity instance is destroyed and a new instance is\ncreated. UI state that exists only in views or Activity fields can\ntherefore be lost. The usual Android approach is to separate durable UI\nstate from the Activity instance and restore it through the appropriate\nstate mechanism.\n\nFor small transient values, onSaveInstanceState() can save data in a\nBundle, which Android supplies to the recreated Activity. For screen\nstate that should survive configuration changes and is owned by the\nUI/business layer, a ViewModel is normally preferred because the\nViewModel survives Activity recreation. For larger or\nprocess-recreation-sensitive state, persistent storage such as\nSavedStateHandle, Room, or DataStore may be appropriate depending on the\nrequirement.\n\nThe key is to distinguish configuration-change survival from\nprocess-death survival. A ViewModel helps with configuration changes but\nshould not be treated as permanent persistence.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nSource Level: Junior / Entry-Level\n\nAnswer:\nWhen an Activity is recreated after a configuration change such\nas rotation, the Activity instance is destroyed and a new instance is\ncreated. UI state that exists only in views or Activity fields can\ntherefore be lost. The usual Android approach is to separate durable UI\nstate from the Activity instance and restore it through the appropriate\nstate mechanism.\n\nFor small transient values, onSaveInstanceState() can save data in a\nBundle, which Android supplies to the recreated Activity. For screen\nstate that should survive configuration changes and is owned by the\nUI/business layer, a ViewModel is normally preferred because the\nViewModel survives Activity recreation. For larger or\nprocess-recreation-sensitive state, persistent storage such as\nSavedStateHandle, Room, or DataStore may be appropriate depending on the\nrequirement.\n\nThe key is to distinguish configuration-change survival from\nprocess-death survival. A ViewModel helps with configuration changes but\nshould not be treated as permanent persistence.\n\nExample:\nclass DetailsActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\n        private lateinit var viewModel: DetailsViewModel\n\n        override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n            super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n            viewModel = ViewModelProvider(this)[DetailsViewModel::class.java]\n\n            savedInstanceState?.getString(\"selectedSector\")?.let {\n                viewModel.selectSector(it)\n            }\n        }\n\n        override fun onSaveInstanceState(outState: Bundle) {\n            outState.putString(\"selectedSector\", viewModel.selectedSector.value)\n            super.onSaveInstanceState(outState)\n        }\n    }\n\nFor production code, the exact state mechanism should match whether the\nstate must survive configuration change, process death, or app restart.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nKnow the definition, lifecycle/runtime\nbehavior, practical use, trade-offs, and common failure modes.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Snapmint, SDE-1 Android, July 2026.\nThe candidate describes a question involving retaining the selected\nsector when an Activity is recreated after a configuration change.",
    "explanation": "In an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Snapmint, SDE-1 Android, July 2026.\nThe candidate describes a question involving retaining the selected\nsector when an Activity is recreated after a configuration change.",
