    "question": "How do Fragment lifecycle transitions work across different navigation scenarios?",
    "slug": "android-developer-how-do-fragment-lifecycle-transitions-work-across-different-navigation-scenarios-2",
    "shortDescription": "Reported Android interview question for junior / entry-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "A Fragment has its own lifecycle that is coordinated with, but\ndistinct from, its host Activity. A typical Fragment moves through\nstates such as onAttach(), onCreate(), onCreateView(), onViewCreated(),\nonStart(), and onResume(), and reverses through onPause(), onStop(),\nonDestroyView(), and onDestroy() as appropriate.\n\nNavigation matters because the Fragment’s view lifecycle can end while\nthe Fragment object itself remains in the FragmentManager back stack.\nThat is why view-related resources and view binding should normally be\nreleased in onDestroyView() rather than onDestroy().\n\nFor nested fragments, back-stack operations, configuration changes, and\ntransitions, the exact callbacks depend on what is being added, removed,\nhidden, shown, or replaced. A strong answer therefore explains the\nlifecycle as a state transition rather than memorizing callback order.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nSource Level: Junior / Entry-Level\n\nAnswer:\nA Fragment has its own lifecycle that is coordinated with, but\ndistinct from, its host Activity. A typical Fragment moves through\nstates such as onAttach(), onCreate(), onCreateView(), onViewCreated(),\nonStart(), and onResume(), and reverses through onPause(), onStop(),\nonDestroyView(), and onDestroy() as appropriate.\n\nNavigation matters because the Fragment’s view lifecycle can end while\nthe Fragment object itself remains in the FragmentManager back stack.\nThat is why view-related resources and view binding should normally be\nreleased in onDestroyView() rather than onDestroy().\n\nFor nested fragments, back-stack operations, configuration changes, and\ntransitions, the exact callbacks depend on what is being added, removed,\nhidden, shown, or replaced. A strong answer therefore explains the\nlifecycle as a state transition rather than memorizing callback order.\n\nExample:\noverride fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n        super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState)\n\n        viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope.launch {\n            viewLifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n                viewModel.uiState.collect { state ->\n                    render(state)\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nFragment lifecycle and view lifecycle are\ndifferent; release view resources in onDestroyView().\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Snapmint, SDE-1 Android, July 2026.\nThe candidate explicitly reports being asked about Fragment lifecycle\ntransitions.",
