    "question": "What are log interceptors and how would you use them in an Android networking stack?",
    "slug": "android-developer-what-are-log-interceptors-and-how-would-you-use-them-in-an-android-networking-stack-10",
    "shortDescription": "Reported topic Android interview question for mid-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "A logging interceptor sits in the networking stack and observes\noutgoing requests and incoming responses. With an HTTP client such as\nOkHttp, an interceptor can record method, URL, timing, status code, and\nselected headers for debugging.\n\nLogging must be environment-aware. Development builds may use detailed\nlogs, while production should avoid sensitive data such as authorization\nheaders, tokens, passwords, and personal information. An interceptor can\nalso help measure request duration and diagnose failures without\nchanging application business logic.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED TOPIC]\n\nSource Level: Mid-Level\n\nAnswer:\nA logging interceptor sits in the networking stack and observes\noutgoing requests and incoming responses. With an HTTP client such as\nOkHttp, an interceptor can record method, URL, timing, status code, and\nselected headers for debugging.\n\nLogging must be environment-aware. Development builds may use detailed\nlogs, while production should avoid sensitive data such as authorization\nheaders, tokens, passwords, and personal information. An interceptor can\nalso help measure request duration and diagnose failures without\nchanging application business logic.\n\nExample:\nval client = OkHttpClient.Builder()\n        .addInterceptor { chain ->\n            val request = chain.request()\n            val start = System.nanoTime()\n            val response = chain.proceed(request)\n            val elapsedMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000\n            logger.debug(\"${request.method} ${request.url} -> ${response.code} (${elapsedMs}ms)\")\n            response\n        }\n        .build()\n\nProduction logging should redact authorization headers, cookies, tokens,\nand sensitive bodies.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nHandle cancellation, timeouts, HTTP errors,\nparsing, retries, security, and observability.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Target, Software Engineer - Android.\nThe candidate explicitly reports being asked about log interceptors and\nnetworking issues.",
