    "question": "Given coroutine code using launch, delay, and Thread.sleep, what output will it produce and in what order?",
    "slug": "android-developer-given-coroutine-code-using-launch-delay-and-thread-sleep-what-output-will-it-produce-and-in-what-ord-11",
    "shortDescription": "Reported Android interview question for mid-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "The crucial distinction is that delay() is a coroutine\nsuspension point, whereas Thread.sleep() blocks the current thread.\nlaunch starts a coroutine and returns a Job; the actual ordering depends\non the dispatcher, scheduling, and whether code is running sequentially\nin the same coroutine or concurrently.\n\nWhen answering an output question, do not assume that coroutine creation\nautomatically means parallel execution. Trace each coroutine, identify\nits dispatcher/thread, mark suspension points, and account for blocking\ncalls. If multiple coroutines can race, state explicitly whether the\noutput is deterministic or scheduling-dependent.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nSource Level: Mid-Level\n\nAnswer:\nThe crucial distinction is that delay() is a coroutine\nsuspension point, whereas Thread.sleep() blocks the current thread.\nlaunch starts a coroutine and returns a Job; the actual ordering depends\non the dispatcher, scheduling, and whether code is running sequentially\nin the same coroutine or concurrently.\n\nWhen answering an output question, do not assume that coroutine creation\nautomatically means parallel execution. Trace each coroutine, identify\nits dispatcher/thread, mark suspension points, and account for blocking\ncalls. If multiple coroutines can race, state explicitly whether the\noutput is deterministic or scheduling-dependent.\n\nExample:\nrunBlocking {\n        launch {\n            delay(100)\n            println(\"A\")\n        }\n        launch {\n            Thread.sleep(50)\n            println(\"B\")\n        }\n        println(\"C\")\n    }\n\nThe exact order must be reasoned from coroutine scheduling and blocking\nbehavior; Thread.sleep blocks its thread while delay suspends its\ncoroutine.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nKnow the definition, lifecycle/runtime\nbehavior, practical use, trade-offs, and common failure modes.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: JioCinema, SDE-2 Android, June 2026.\nThe candidate was explicitly asked to determine the output of a\ncoroutine program containing delay and Thread.sleep.",
    "explanation": "In an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: JioCinema, SDE-2 Android, June 2026.\nThe candidate was explicitly asked to determine the output of a\ncoroutine program containing delay and Thread.sleep.",
    "keyPoints": "Know the definition, lifecycle/runtime\nbehavior, practical use, trade-offs, and common failure modes.",
    "commonMistakes": "Giving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.",
