    "question": "How would you write a suspend function that makes multiple API calls and returns the required results, while failing if either call fails?",
    "slug": "android-developer-how-would-you-write-a-suspend-function-that-makes-multiple-api-calls-and-returns-the-required-result-12",
    "shortDescription": "Reported Android interview question for mid-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "Use a suspend function and make the failure policy explicit. If\nboth API calls are independent and both results are required,\ncoroutineScope with async is a natural approach: start both operations\nconcurrently, then await() both results. If either operation fails,\nstructured concurrency propagates the failure and cancels the sibling\nwork when appropriate.\n\nFor example, two required profile-related endpoints can be requested\nconcurrently and combined into one result. If one failure should not\ncancel the other, that is a different requirement and may call for\nsupervisorScope or explicit error handling.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nSource Level: Mid-Level\n\nAnswer:\nUse a suspend function and make the failure policy explicit. If\nboth API calls are independent and both results are required,\ncoroutineScope with async is a natural approach: start both operations\nconcurrently, then await() both results. If either operation fails,\nstructured concurrency propagates the failure and cancels the sibling\nwork when appropriate.\n\nFor example, two required profile-related endpoints can be requested\nconcurrently and combined into one result. If one failure should not\ncancel the other, that is a different requirement and may call for\nsupervisorScope or explicit error handling.\n\nExample:\nsuspend fun loadProfile(): Profile {\n        return api.getProfile()\n    }\n\nCalling loadProfile() suspends the calling coroutine while the\nsuspendable operation is in progress; it does not inherently create a\nnew thread.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nSuspend ≠ background thread; suspension avoids\nblocking; dispatcher controls execution context.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: JioCinema, SDE-2 Android, June 2026.\nThe candidate explicitly reports a final technical question requiring a\nsuspend function around multiple API calls and error handling.",
    "explanation": "In an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: JioCinema, SDE-2 Android, June 2026.\nThe candidate explicitly reports a final technical question requiring a\nsuspend function around multiple API calls and error handling.",
    "keyPoints": "Suspend ≠ background thread; suspension avoids\nblocking; dispatcher controls execution context.",
    "commonMistakes": "Giving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.",
    "followUpQuestions": "Why would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?",
