    "question": "Explain how you solved ANRs in a previous Android project.",
    "slug": "android-developer-explain-how-you-solved-anrs-in-a-previous-android-project-18",
    "shortDescription": "Reported topic Android interview question for mid-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "An ANR occurs when an application fails to respond within\nAndroid’s responsiveness expectations, commonly because the main thread\nis blocked for too long. Typical causes include disk or network I/O,\nexpensive computation, lock contention, or poorly designed\ninitialization on the main thread.\n\nI would reproduce and capture traces, inspect main-thread stack\ninformation, identify blocking work, move appropriate work off the main\nthread using structured concurrency or suitable APIs, and reduce\nexpensive UI operations. The fix should be validated with profiling and\na regression test or monitoring where practical.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED TOPIC]\n\nSource Level: Mid-Level\n\nAnswer:\nAn ANR occurs when an application fails to respond within\nAndroid’s responsiveness expectations, commonly because the main thread\nis blocked for too long. Typical causes include disk or network I/O,\nexpensive computation, lock contention, or poorly designed\ninitialization on the main thread.\n\nI would reproduce and capture traces, inspect main-thread stack\ninformation, identify blocking work, move appropriate work off the main\nthread using structured concurrency or suitable APIs, and reduce\nexpensive UI operations. The fix should be validated with profiling and\na regression test or monitoring where practical.\n\nExample:\nviewModelScope.launch {\n        val result = withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {\n            repository.loadLargeDataset()\n        }\n        _uiState.value = UiState.Success(result)\n    }\n\nThe expensive I/O work is moved away from the main thread, while the\nViewModel owns the coroutine lifetime.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nProtect the main thread; find blocking work;\nuse profiling/traces; fix the root cause.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Fincart, Mobile Applications\nDeveloper, 2026. The candidate explicitly reports project discussion\nabout how ANRs were solved.",
    "explanation": "In an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Fincart, Mobile Applications\nDeveloper, 2026. The candidate explicitly reports project discussion\nabout how ANRs were solved.",
    "keyPoints": "Protect the main thread; find blocking work;\nuse profiling/traces; fix the root cause.",
    "commonMistakes": "Giving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.",
