    "slug": "android-developer-explain-kotlin-coroutines-flow-jetpack-compose-and-mvvm-19",
    "shortDescription": "Reported topic Android interview question for mid-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "Coroutines provide structured asynchronous execution; Flow\nrepresents asynchronous streams of values; Compose renders UI\ndeclaratively from state; and MVVM commonly places screen state and\nevent handling in a ViewModel.\n\nA robust design can expose a StateFlow<UiState> from the ViewModel,\ncollect it from the UI with lifecycle-aware APIs, and use suspend\nfunctions for one-shot operations. The important relationship is\nunidirectional data flow: user events go toward the state owner, and\nstate flows back to the UI.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED TOPIC]\n\nSource Level: Mid-Level\n\nAnswer:\nCoroutines provide structured asynchronous execution; Flow\nrepresents asynchronous streams of values; Compose renders UI\ndeclaratively from state; and MVVM commonly places screen state and\nevent handling in a ViewModel.\n\nA robust design can expose a StateFlow<UiState> from the ViewModel,\ncollect it from the UI with lifecycle-aware APIs, and use suspend\nfunctions for one-shot operations. The important relationship is\nunidirectional data flow: user events go toward the state owner, and\nstate flows back to the UI.\n\nExample:\nExample: Start with a small Android feature, identify the\nstate owner and lifecycle, implement the simplest correct solution, then\nadd error handling and tests for the important edge cases.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nCompose is state-driven and declarative; keep\nside effects controlled and state ownership explicit.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Target, Software Engineer - Android.\nThe candidate explicitly lists these subjects as part of the interview.",
