    "question": "How do you use StateFlow and SharedFlow, and why would you choose one over the other?",
    "slug": "android-developer-how-do-you-use-stateflow-and-sharedflow-and-why-would-you-choose-one-over-the-other-20",
    "shortDescription": "Reported topic Android interview question for mid-level candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "StateFlow represents observable state and always has a current\nvalue. New collectors receive the latest state immediately. It is\ntherefore well suited to persistent UI state such as loading, content,\nor error state.\n\nSharedFlow represents a hot stream of shared emissions and is useful\nwhen consumers need events rather than a single current state—for\nexample, navigation commands or one-time UI notifications. The choice\nshould follow semantics: use StateFlow for state, and SharedFlow for\nbroadcast-style events where replay/buffering behavior is configured\ndeliberately.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED TOPIC]\n\nSource Level: Mid-Level\n\nAnswer:\nStateFlow represents observable state and always has a current\nvalue. New collectors receive the latest state immediately. It is\ntherefore well suited to persistent UI state such as loading, content,\nor error state.\n\nSharedFlow represents a hot stream of shared emissions and is useful\nwhen consumers need events rather than a single current state—for\nexample, navigation commands or one-time UI notifications. The choice\nshould follow semantics: use StateFlow for state, and SharedFlow for\nbroadcast-style events where replay/buffering behavior is configured\ndeliberately.\n\nExample:\nprivate val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(UiState.Loading)\n    val uiState: StateFlow<UiState> = _uiState\n\n    private val _events = MutableSharedFlow<UiEvent>()\n    val events = _events.asSharedFlow()\n\nuiState represents current screen state; events can represent transient\nbroadcasts such as navigation or a snackbar request.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nStateFlow = current state; SharedFlow = shared\nemissions/events; choose based on semantics.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Senior Android Developer interviews\nreported on Glassdoor, 2026. The report explicitly identifies StateFlow\nvs SharedFlow as a deep-level Android interview topic.\n============================================================ SENIOR /\nADVANCED — REPORTED\n============================================================",
    "explanation": "In an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Senior Android Developer interviews\nreported on Glassdoor, 2026. The report explicitly identifies StateFlow\nvs SharedFlow as a deep-level Android interview topic.\n============================================================ SENIOR /\nADVANCED — REPORTED\n============================================================",
    "keyPoints": "StateFlow = current state; SharedFlow = shared\nemissions/events; choose based on semantics.",
    "commonMistakes": "Giving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.",
    "followUpQuestions": "Why would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?",
