    "question": "If a ViewModel uses SupervisorJob, how do failures propagate?",
    "slug": "android-developer-if-a-viewmodel-uses-supervisorjob-how-do-failures-propagate-22",
    "shortDescription": "Reported Android interview question for senior / advanced candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "A SupervisorJob changes sibling failure behavior. With a normal\nparent Job, an uncaught child failure can cancel the parent and its\nother children. With a SupervisorJob, failure of one child does not\nautomatically cancel its sibling children.\n\nThis is useful when independent operations should continue even if one\nfails. However, supervisor semantics do not mean errors disappear: an\nuncaught exception still needs appropriate handling, and the supervisor\nitself can still be cancelled by its parent.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nSource Level: Senior / Advanced\n\nAnswer:\nA SupervisorJob changes sibling failure behavior. With a normal\nparent Job, an uncaught child failure can cancel the parent and its\nother children. With a SupervisorJob, failure of one child does not\nautomatically cancel its sibling children.\n\nThis is useful when independent operations should continue even if one\nfails. However, supervisor semantics do not mean errors disappear: an\nuncaught exception still needs appropriate handling, and the supervisor\nitself can still be cancelled by its parent.\n\nExample:\nval scope = CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + Dispatchers.IO)\n\n    scope.launch {\n        throw RuntimeException(\"first task failed\")\n    }\n\n    scope.launch {\n        // This sibling can continue because the parent is supervised.\n    }\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nSupervisorJob isolates sibling failures;\ncancellation and exception handling still matter.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Senior Android Developer interview\nreported on Glassdoor, 2026. The reported interview question explicitly\nconcerns SupervisorJob and failure propagation.",
    "explanation": "In an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Senior Android Developer interview\nreported on Glassdoor, 2026. The reported interview question explicitly\nconcerns SupervisorJob and failure propagation.",
    "keyPoints": "SupervisorJob isolates sibling failures;\ncancellation and exception handling still matter.",
    "commonMistakes": "Giving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.",
