    "question": "How would you handle a state-related bug in a Compose/MVVM application?",
    "slug": "android-developer-how-would-you-handle-a-state-related-bug-in-a-compose-mvvm-application-29",
    "shortDescription": "Reported topic Android interview question for senior / advanced candidates.",
    "sampleAnswer": "First identify which state is wrong, who owns it, and whether\nthere are multiple competing sources of truth. In a Compose/MVVM\napplication, UI state should normally have a clear owner, often a\nViewModel, while Composables render that state and emit user events.\n\nI would reproduce the issue, inspect state transitions, check whether\nstate is being overwritten by multiple collectors or asynchronous\noperations, and verify lifecycle behavior. Then I would establish a\nsingle source of truth, make state updates explicit, and add a\nregression test.\n\nFor asynchronous state, I would also check cancellation, stale\nresponses, configuration changes, and whether a collector is observing\nthe correct lifecycle.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: [REPORTED TOPIC]\n\nSource Level: Senior / Advanced\n\nAnswer:\nFirst identify which state is wrong, who owns it, and whether\nthere are multiple competing sources of truth. In a Compose/MVVM\napplication, UI state should normally have a clear owner, often a\nViewModel, while Composables render that state and emit user events.\n\nI would reproduce the issue, inspect state transitions, check whether\nstate is being overwritten by multiple collectors or asynchronous\noperations, and verify lifecycle behavior. Then I would establish a\nsingle source of truth, make state updates explicit, and add a\nregression test.\n\nFor asynchronous state, I would also check cancellation, stale\nresponses, configuration changes, and whether a collector is observing\nthe correct lifecycle.\n\nExample:\ndata class UiState(\n        val selectedId: String? = null,\n        val loading: Boolean = false,\n        val error: String? = null\n    )\n\n    class ScreenViewModel : ViewModel() {\n        private val _state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\n        val state = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n        fun select(id: String) {\n            _state.update { it.copy(selectedId = id) }\n        }\n    }\n\nThe ViewModel owns the state, while the Composable renders it and sends\nuser actions back.\n\nInterview Tip:\nIn an interview, define the concept first, explain the\nruntime/lifecycle behavior, then give a concrete Android example and\nmention the main trade-off or edge case.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nThe interviewer is testing whether you\nunderstand the concept deeply enough to apply it in a real Android\napplication, not just recall terminology.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nCompose is state-driven and declarative; keep\nside effects controlled and state ownership explicit.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\ncan go wrong? How does it behave across lifecycle changes? How would you\ntest it?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a definition without explaining behavior;\nconfusing lifecycle/configuration change with process death; ignoring\ncancellation, threading, lifecycle ownership, or error handling.\n\nSource Evidence:\nInterview report: Target, Software Engineer - Android.\nThe candidate reports a case study containing state-related bugs and a\ntechnical discussion involving Compose and MVVM.",
